  <h1>What is the Qixi Festival?</h1>
  <div class="zh">七夕</div>
  <p>A seventh-night festival shaped by a love story in the stars and by older traditions of asking for skill.</p>
  <p style="color:#6a6153;font-size:13px;margin-top:10px">By <a href="/editorial-policy" style="color:inherit">yaopulife Editorial</a> · Published 2026-07-26 · Updated 2026-10-22</p>
</div></header>

<main class="wrap">
<p style="color:#6a6153;font-size:13px;margin:18px 0"><a href="/">Home</a> · <a href="/festivals">Festivals</a> · Qixi Festival</p>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="answer">The quick answer</h2>
  <p class="lead" style="max-width:780px"><b>Qixi</b> (<span class="zh">七夕</span> <i>Qīxī</i>) falls on the <b>seventh day of the seventh month</b> in the traditional Chinese lunisolar calendar. It is linked with the myth of the Cowherd and Weaver Girl, who meet across the Milky Way once a year. Yet the festival is more than a romance: it is also known as <b>Qiqiao</b> (<span class="zh">乞巧</span> <i>qǐqiǎo</i>), “asking for skill,” after customs through which women and girls sought dexterity.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="legend">The Cowherd and Weaver Girl</h2>
  <p>In a common version of the myth, Niulang (<span class="zh">牛郎</span>), the Cowherd, and Zhinü (<span class="zh">织女</span>), the Weaver Girl, are separated by the Milky Way. Magpies form a bridge so they can reunite on the seventh night of the seventh lunar month.</p>
  <p>Institutional accounts do not tell every detail in exactly the same way. The stable core is the annual meeting of the two figures; other plot details belong to different versions of a folktale, not to confirmed history.</p>
  <div class="note"><span class="nlbl">Calendar note</span><b>“Seventh month” means the lunar month.</b> Qixi is not fixed to July 7 on the Gregorian calendar, so its Western-calendar date changes each year.</div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="qiqiao">Why Qixi is also the “festival of asking for skill”</h2>
  <p>The Chinese Intangible Cultural Heritage project record identifies Qixi as both <i>Qiqiao Festival</i> and a festival historically important to women. It records customs that vary by region, including worship connected with the Cowherd and Weaver Girl, skill-seeking activities, and objects associated with “asking for cleverness.”</p>
  <p>A Palace Museum account gives a more concrete scene: fruit offerings in a courtyard beneath the night sky, with women competing to thread needles. These records support the festival's link with dexterity and women's work without suggesting that every household followed one fixed ritual.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="valentines-day">Why “Chinese Valentine's Day” is only shorthand</h2>
  <p>Calling Qixi “Chinese Valentine's Day” points to the Cowherd and Weaver Girl love story and to romance associated with the day. It can be a useful introduction, but it narrows a festival whose documented traditions also include prayer, craft, skill and women's aspirations.</p>
  <p>That older dimension is not only archival. An Associated Press report from Beijing's Folk Custom Museum in 2026 described families taking part in craft activities inspired by <i>qiqiao</i>, including making decorative <i>qiaoguo</i> pastries. The example shows how institutions can present historical practices today; it does not make one museum program a universal modern custom.</p>
</section>

